WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.939 --> 00:00:11.123
 Studying anything at all that has a kind of distance from our moment is the thing

00:00:11.123 --> 00:00:17.144
 that will actually give you some perspective on what's happening right now.

00:00:17.144 --> 00:00:23.930
 Any kind of cultural context that has some foreignness, some strangeness

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:29.650
 will then by comparison bring certain aspects of our own moment in high relief.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:30.720
 I'm Feisal Mohamed.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.936
 I'm a professor of English specializing in early modern English literature

00:00:34.936 --> 00:00:38.159
  especially people like Hobbes, Milton, and Marvel.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.912
 The 17th century was the time when in so many ways the modern world took shape.

00:00:44.912 --> 00:00:48.244
 This is a period of mass social upheaval.

00:00:48.244 --> 00:00:50.659
 It's a period of mass migration.

00:00:50.659 --> 00:00:52.688
 It's a period of civil war.

00:00:52.688 --> 00:00:57.918
 It's a period when the king was executed and England began a great political experiment.

00:00:57.918 --> 00:01:01.000
 And that's the setting of the writers I study.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.720
 Milton was not just a great poet, this titanic figure in English poetry.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.830
 But also he was a great poet-revolutionary.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:16.078
 He was the first English thinker to defend in print the execution of King Charles I.

00:01:16.078 --> 00:01:26.209
 Studying Milton draws us out of casual assumptions we might have about what it is that poetry does.

00:01:28.912 --> 00:01:35.167
 We tend now to think of poetry as beautiful and useless, to borrow a title.

00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:37.251
 That's not at all how Milton thought of poetry.

00:01:37.251 --> 00:01:40.126
 For Milton it was both beautiful and transformative.

00:01:40.126 --> 00:01:46.310
 The way that Milton knows intimately the entire European literary tradition leading up to him...

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:51.386
 Everyone from Homer and Virgil up to writers of his own moment...

00:01:51.386 --> 00:01:54.449
 The amount of knowledge that he had was simply staggering.

00:01:54.449 --> 00:02:01.248
 And he can draw on all of it and use it as a weapon as it were to write a work of literature

00:02:01.248 --> 00:02:03.334
 that defiantly says

00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:09.000
 all of the other epic poets conceived of the project of epic poetry incorrectly.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.126
 This is the epic truly married to truth.

00:02:12.126 --> 00:02:19.149
 And to use that truth as a weapon against the restored English monarchy, the restored English church...

00:02:19.149 --> 00:02:25.751
 That's a sort of energy within a work like Paradise Lost that I find endlessly fascinating.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:32.960
 There's no other great poet who had Milton's revolutionary investments.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.850
 There's no other revolutionary who is so great a poet.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:40.250
 Yes, he was a radical.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:45.370
 No, we would not want to join him in his particular radical vision.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:49.026
 And this is where one's relationship to Milton gets complicated.

00:02:49.026 --> 00:02:56.380
 You can admire his revolutionary energy even as you see that the particular revolution

00:02:56.380 --> 00:03:00.359
 he was fighting for is not one you would want to join.

00:03:00.359 --> 00:03:08.370
 What Milton really wanted looks to us now like an oligarchy of white male protestants.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:11.660
 So the content of his ideas we would want to disown.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.410
 And yet the activist energy you can still admire

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:17.084
 especially in its relationship to poetic achievement.

00:03:17.084 --> 00:03:19.167
 You can't divorce one from the other.

00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:22.892
 And I think that we can hold those two things in our head at the same time.

00:03:22.892 --> 00:03:28.292
 We can say this is brilliant even as we say this guy was kind of a jerk.

00:03:28.292 --> 00:03:32.743
 And yet I still admire him as a great writer and thinker.

00:03:32.743 --> 00:03:36.860
 I think these writers become a lot more interesting

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:40.459
 when we're willing to look at the good and the bad

00:03:40.459 --> 00:03:44.232
 and to see them in their full complexity.

